  "result": "`python3 -c \"from shop.cli import main; main(['quote','a:12.5:2'])\"` now prints `27.06`, and all 10 tests pass.\n\n**Root cause:** `to_cents` in `shop/money.py:9` padded a one-digit fraction on the left, so `\"12.5\"` became 12.05 instead of 12.50.\n- `frac.rjust(2, \"0\")` turns `\"5\"` into `\"05\"`, which is 5 cents rather than 50.\n- That made the item 1205 cents instead of 1250. Two of them plus 8.25% tax came to 26.09 instead of 27.06.\n- Only the CLI calls `to_cents`, so the pricing code was never at fault. It computed correctly from the wrong input.\n\n**Fix:** I changed `rjust` to `ljust` on that line, so `\"5\"` becomes `\"50\"`.\n\n**Test:** I added `tests/test_money.py`. It covers `\"12.5\"`, `\"12.34\"`, `\"12.05\"`, `\"12\"`, `\"12.\"`, rejecting `\"1.234\"`, and `fmt`. No test exercised `to_cents` before, which is how this got through.\n\nI didn't touch one related problem. `to_cents` mishandles negative amounts: `\"-0.50\"` gives +50, because `int(\"-0\")` is 0 and the sign is lost. Nothing in the CLI path needs negatives today, so I left it.",
